Most work on noise in QSAR modeling assumes Gaussian label noise and evaluates either representations or model architectures in isolation. \citet{Cortes2015} examined 12 algorithms across multiple noise levels but used fixed representations, and studies that compare representations typically use clean data. No work has systematically compared representation and architecture for noise robustness, asked whether probabilistic methods justify their computational overhead under noise, or tested whether findings generalize across corruption patterns and property types. The first half of this thesis therefore asks four questions.\label{sec:intro:questions} First, how much of the variance in predictive performance do representation and model architecture each explain, and does that division change for the performance retained under increasing label noise? Second, what does the choice of model provide under label noise, and how do deterministic and probabilistic models compare? Third, does the type of label noise matter, or only its quantity, both on clean data and on data with inherent experimental noise? Fourth, what is the impact of artificial label noise on a model's uncertainty estimates? In particular, does the estimated uncertainty rise with increasing label noise, and can it identify which labels are corrupted? To answer them we introduce NoiseInject, an open-source Python package for benchmarking noise robustness and uncertainty quantification.

