This chapter asks how several representations can be joined without also joining their irrelevant features. Our answer is the adaptive hybrid representation, a set of at most a fixed number of features, the budget, selected from several base representations by a search for one dataset and one downstream model. The search covers the importance method, the allocation method, the number of representations, the internal evaluator, the budget and the downstream model. Because every selected feature traces back to its representation, each search yields a readable blueprint. We test the method on small molecules, antibodies, reactions, nucleotide sequences and tumor data. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: datasets where the hybrid beats the best single representation; whether preferred settings differ by dataset and model]}.


