The hybrid ranks features by a fitted model's importance scores, as embedded methods do, and keeps a fixed number of the top-ranked ones, as a filter does. Classical methods treat all features as one flat list. The nearest classical relative, the group lasso, keeps or drops predefined groups of variables whole \citep{Yuan2006}, whereas the hybrid splits the budget among representations (``Feature allocation methods''). Because only original features are kept, each traces back to one representation, which makes blueprints possible.

