Some selected features can be read by a chemist. A PDV descriptor has a name, such as molecular weight or logP, an ECFP4 bit can be shown as the atom environment that sets it, and a MACCS key stands for a known substructure. For an antibody the readable unit is a sequence position and its residue, and for gene expression a named gene set, such as a pathway or a chromosome arm. A feature of a learned embedding shows only which representation was useful, not the chemistry (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}).

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
Readable features serve two purposes. Substructures or descriptors that recur among the kept features can point to parts of the molecule that may be involved in binding or activity, a starting point for a chemist rather than a finding. Kept features that make chemical sense for the endpoint support the claim that the selection finds real signal instead of fitting noise. Directly readable inputs are an alternative to explaining a black-box model after the fact \citep{Rudin2019}. A selected feature is predictive but not necessarily causal, and a correlated unselected feature may matter as much. Features should also be read from the fit on the dataset in question, since a blueprint rebuilt on other molecules can select other features.

