The search is given $K$ frozen representation matrices $\mathbf{X}^{(k)} \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times d_k}$, $k = 1, \dots, K$, for the same $n$ samples, whose widths $d_k$ range from tens to thousands of features. It also takes a label vector $\mathbf{y}$, one group label per sample for cross-validation (for example its scaffold), the dataset's metric, the task type, one or more downstream models and a list of budgets. It returns a hybrid of at most $B$ features before any uncapped augmentation, where the budget $B$ is a maximum rather than a target, and a blueprint that records the settings needed to rebuild it.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] paragraph removed, text in cuts file

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% [TRIM2 2026-09-30] LightGBM pre-screen details moved to Additional file app:add:comparator_prescreen; the full-width comparator sentence kept, with its subject restated
% TODO: review, needs manual review
% TODO: REVIEW MODIFICATION
Representations differ in scale, distribution and missingness. Pooling them before splitting would leak validation-set statistics into training, a well-documented source of optimistic bias in feature selection \citep{Ambroise2002, Vabalas2019}, so all preprocessing runs within each cross-validation fold. When a single representation is scored, missing values are imputed with training-fold medians and entirely missing features are dropped. In the hybrid, missing values are set to 0, and every feature, binary bits included, is standardized with training-fold statistics only. Binary features stay 0/1 only when the search scores a single representation to pick the comparator, and representations wider than 5,000 features are pre-screened for that step only (Additional file~\ref{app:add:comparator_prescreen}).

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% [TRIM2 2026-09-30] outer-fold statement reduced to a pointer to 'Splitting rules'; Koenig fold-count example moved to Additional file app:add:fold_counts
% TODO: review, needs manual review
% CODE INPUT
Outer folds follow the splitting rules in ``Datasets and splitting''. A representation learned from the labels is retrained on each outer training fold only. Within that fold, the search runs its own group-aware cross-validation, 5 folds by default. Inside each of its training folds, a deterministic internal split, the first fold of a 5-fold grouped split, holds out about 20\% of the molecules as whole groups. With fewer than five groups, both splits use as many folds as there are groups (Additional file~\ref{app:add:fold_counts}). Importance and standalone scores come from the training fold only, and the outer test molecules score only the finished hybrid.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
For each representation $k$, the importance method gives every feature a score $s^{(k)}_j \ge 0$ on the internal training part, which ranks features within that representation only. The standalone score $r_k$ is the internal evaluator's score, fitted on representation $k$ alone, on the internal held-out part in the dataset's metric. We rescale $r_k$ to $\tilde r_k$ so that 0 means ``no better than a trivial predictor'', which makes the budget split independent of the metric. For Spearman, Pearson, the Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC) and $R^2$, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, r_k)$. For AUROC and the concordance index, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, 2(r_k - 0.5))$. For RMSE, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, 1 - \mathrm{RMSE}_k/\mathrm{RMSE}_0)$, where $\mathrm{RMSE}_0$ is the error of predicting the training mean, and mean absolute error is rescaled likewise against the training median. For accuracy and weighted F1, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, (r_k - r_0)/(1 - r_0))$, where $r_0$ is the score of always predicting the majority training class.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
An optional representation count $N$ keeps the $N$ representations with the highest standalone scores on the fold. Each remaining representation receives a weight $w_k = g(\tilde r_k)$, with $g$ set by the allocation method, and a share $b^*_k = B\, w_k / \sum_l w_l$ features, rounded by largest remainder and capped at its width. Thus $\sum_k b_k = \min(B, \sum_k d_k)$ and $b_k \le d_k$, and a representation with $w_k > 0$ keeps at least one feature. The global pool does not use these shares (see below).

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
Let $S_k$ be the top $b_k$ features of representation $k$ by $s^{(k)}_j$, among those with $s^{(k)}_j > 0$; if none scores above 0, its full $b_k$ is taken. The cut exists because tree importance gives exactly 0 to every feature no tree split on, and filling the budget with those would pick them by their position in the matrix. The hybrid is $\mathbf{H} = [\mathbf{X}^{(1)}_{:,S_1}, \dots, \mathbf{X}^{(K)}_{:,S_K}]$, with $\sum_k |S_k| \le B$, so it can be narrower than its budget.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
A configuration assigns one value to every searched setting. It is scored by fitting the downstream model on the hybrid in each fold of the search and averaging the held-out scores. The winner is picked separately for each pair of downstream model and budget, never across models. Because this score chose among many configurations, it is optimistic \citep{Varma2006, Cawley2010}, so the comparison we report is nested. The whole search runs inside each outer training fold, and the winning settings are rebuilt there and scored once on the untouched outer test fold, both for the winner at each budget and for each downstream model's best across budgets. The inner search score exceeds the outer test score by \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: inner minus outer test score, per dataset, in its own metric]}.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
% CODE INPUT
Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} uses two variants of this search. Under label noise, training quantities use the noisy labels and held-out scoring uses clean labels unless stated otherwise. Under the nearest-neighbor objective, each molecule's activity is predicted from its five nearest neighbors in the feature space (Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}, ``Similarity in a hybrid'').

