Donors are set per dataset (Augmentation column of Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}). They range from small fingerprints such as MACCS to structural sets such as 3D pharmacophores and Coulomb matrix eigenvalues, and include features that describe only differences between variants, such as antibody mutation features. MACCS is a donor on four of the six small-molecule datasets and a base representation on MDR1--MDCK efflux. On QM9, MACCS is not offered and the topological torsion fingerprint (2,048 features) is a donor, the only wide one. \citet{Bahia2023} found that combining 2D and 3D descriptors gave many more significant models across six protein--ligand series (461 structures), which they attribute to complementary properties. Their 3D descriptors came from bioactive conformations in the Protein Data Bank, whereas our 3D donors use one generated conformer per molecule.
% TODO: add augmentation subsection

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
Donor features are ranked by what the base gets wrong. Inside each outer training fold, an 80/20 internal split keeps each scaffold or sequence group on one side. A small random forest (50 trees, depth at most 10) is fitted on the base features, and 3-fold group-aware cross-validation gives out-of-fold residuals $r_i = y_i - \hat{y}^{\mathrm{oof}}_i$ for the internal-training molecules, where for binary tasks $\hat{y}$ is the positive-class probability. In-sample residuals would shrink toward zero. All donor features are pooled, each is scored by its absolute Pearson correlation with the residuals, $c_j = |\rho_{\mathrm{P}}(\mathbf{x}_j, \mathbf{r})|$, and the top $M = 50$ go forward. Ranking against $\mathbf{y}$ would favor features that repeat what the base captures; ranking against $\mathbf{r}$ favors features that explain its errors, as in gradient boosting \citep{friedman2001greedy} and least angle regression \citep{efron2004lars}.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
Blocks of the top $k \in \{0, 5, 10, 20, 40\}$ ranked features, capped at the number of candidates, are each added to the base and scored with the downstream model on the internal-validation molecules. The chosen $k^*$ has the best score and must beat $k=0$, which wins ties, so keeping no extras is the default. Five block choices instead of about 50 accept-or-reject decisions leave less room to overfit a small validation set \citep{Cawley2010}. The chosen donors and feature positions are stored and re-extracted for the outer fold. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: how often $k^*=0$, and per-fold scores for no, uncapped and capped augmentation, per dataset and downstream model]}

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
In uncapped mode, extras are appended outside the budget, so the final width is $|\mathbf{H}| + k^*$, at most $B + 40$ features. In capped mode, they displace base features and the width stays at most $B$. The base features are ranked once by random forest importance (50 trees, depth at most 10) and the $k^*$ weakest are dropped, after any unspent budget is filled, keeping at least one base feature. Uncapped augmentation and no augmentation differ in width at the same $B$, so their comparison mixes better features with more features. Capped mode, where an extra must beat a base feature, removes this difference.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
% CODE INPUT
Augmentation is a separate condition rather than a searched setting, so each dataset with donors is searched three times, with no, uncapped and capped augmentation. Fourteen of the 16 datasets run all three, while humanness and trastuzumab--HER2 binding have no donors. The three conditions also run under the nearest-neighbor objective, on the datasets given in Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}, ``Similarity in a hybrid''. In a second use, a user keeps one strong representation at full width as the base and adds a few extras, with $B$ its width, so only uncapped mode makes sense. We test this use, with planted decoy features, in ``Comparators, controls and ablations''. Augmentation also asks directly whether another representation holds signal the base lacks (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}). On Koenig expression, the mutation features and the ESM-2 log-likelihood ratios reach the model only through augmentation. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: donors kept most often, per domain]}

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
The residual ranking has four limitations. $|\rho_{\mathrm{P}}|$ captures only linear relations, one feature at a time. It ignores redundancy, so two near-identical donor features can both rank high. Donors are pooled with no per-donor limit, so a 2,048-feature donor gets more chances than a 49-feature one. Finally, the margin over $k=0$ is zero, so a near-tie on one internal split can admit extras.

