% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
The search can be made cheaper in two ways. The fast tier uses shorter value lists for some settings, fixed in advance and identical on every fast-tier dataset, and accepts a possibly worse answer for a much smaller grid. Adaptive pruning decides during the search from the dataset's own fold scores, and aims to find the same best configuration while no longer scoring configurations that have clearly lost. Without either, the full search does not finish on the largest datasets.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
Six of the 16 datasets use the fast tier, namely QM9, LogD, Koenig expression, trastuzumab--HER2 binding, H3K4me3 and TATA promoter (marked in Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}). A dataset uses it when the full search would take longer than about 40 hours, the time four consecutive 12-hour jobs cover, or when it has more than 10,000 molecules or sequences. Estimated full-search run times were about 103 hours for QM9, 64 for Koenig expression, 62 for LogD and 39 for TATA promoter, and H3K4me3 has 18,244 sequences. The values the fast tier drops are still searched on every other dataset, so it is a compromise on compute, not a claim that those values are useless.

