Because votes are grouped by every other setting, a value that is best for a random forest at 25 features is not removed for losing to a DNN at 500 features. One fold is not enough. In the representation-count vote on breast cancer PAM50 subtype after fold 1 alone, 726 of 1,020 groups had every value at an identical score, and ties broken by list order then remove values by accident. After one fold, no cut-off of any vote was safe on the calibration datasets, whereas after two folds each vote had at least one safe cut-off.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
% CODE INPUT
The cut-offs were calibrated in a preliminary study on five datasets, LogD, hERG $K_i$, breast cancer PAM50 subtype, nanobody thermostability and humanness, by replaying each rule on fold scores from an unpruned five-fold run. A cut-off counts as safe if the best five-fold score is unchanged for each of the 25 combinations of downstream model and budget, on every dataset. Among safe cut-offs that made the same cuts, we chose the one whose neighboring cut-off was also safe. TreeSHAP accounts for 71\% of LogD's fitting time and was never the best there, so most of the saving comes from it. Estimated run times for eight budgets, without and with pruning, were 79 and 44 hours on LogD, 42 and 35 on hERG $K_i$, 42 and 30 on PAM50 subtype, 32 and 24 on nanobody thermostability, and 15 and 13 on humanness. The calibration applied the votes one after another, whereas the search applies all four at once to the fold 1 and 2 scores. Five datasets form a small calibration set, which limits the rules.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
With pruning on, folds 1 and 2 both select the survivors and score them, so the winner's five-fold mean is slightly optimistic \citep{Cawley2010}. Single representations are never pruned. A fair comparison with a single representation therefore leaves both voting folds out of both scores, or re-scores without them.

