\section{Discussion}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]
% - TODO(lit): neither source above is a study of when combining gives no gain. A source that reports it directly is still needed.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% TODO: review, needs manual review
\citet{Ambroise2002} showed the extreme case of overfitting through feature selection. An SVM reached near-zero cross-validated error on random labels when genes were selected on the full dataset first. With selection nested inside each fold, the median error was 11 out of 40, close to chance. The bias from pre-selection can exceed that from hyperparameter tuning \citep{Vabalas2019}. The hybrid search computes all importance scores within each fold, on training data only, but searching over many feature subsets still adds complexity \citep{Tibshirani2015}. The budget acts as a hard capacity constraint: selecting 100 features from 5,000 constrains the possible selections far more tightly than $L_1$ regularization, because the feasible set grows polynomially in the budget while a regularized model's parameter space is unbounded \citep{Ng2004}. The selected features are also frozen embeddings rather than fitted parameters, and we consider choosing among them less expressive than learning new features. In support, frozen embeddings with a linear probe can outperform full fine-tuning by up to 10\% in out-of-distribution accuracy \citep{Kumar2022}. Finally, overfit selection would give feature sets that differ widely between folds, which the Nogueira index measures (``Comparators, controls and ablations'').

