We use hybrids for similarity in two ways. In an ordinary hybrid, the search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} selects features that help a downstream model, and distances are computed on those features. A similarity hybrid comes from the same search, but each candidate is ranked by how accurately each molecule's activity is predicted from its five nearest neighbors in the hybrid, and no downstream model is fitted. The two searches share importance methods, allocation methods, budgets and folds. The neighbor score, computed on a validation set from the fold's training molecules, also replaces the internal evaluator in splitting the budget. All of the dataset's representations are candidates, and the number of representations is not searched. The similarity hybrid tests whether choosing features for the quality of the neighbors addresses the problems of Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} more directly than choosing them for prediction. \textbf{[FIGURE NEEDED: D6.1, one search, two scores]}

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
The distance is the Manhattan distance of Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} on features z-scored as in Chapter~\ref{chap:method}. Each z-scored feature adds a term of similar size to this distance, so a representation's weight in the distance, set by the allocation method, is roughly its number of features divided by the budget. The neighbor score applies no importance weighting. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: confirm Manhattan distance and inverse-distance weighting]}

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
To rank whole candidates, the score is computed on each outer fold's held-out molecules, which also serve as each other's neighbors. Their scaffold groups are split into up to 5 parts, and each molecule's 5 nearest neighbors are drawn from the other parts, because a neighbor with the same scaffold is an easy match that would inflate the score. Each neighbor is weighted by the inverse of its distance:
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}_i = \frac{\sum_{j\in N_5(i)} y_j / d_{ij}}{\sum_{j\in N_5(i)} 1/d_{ij}}.
\end{equation}
On regression data, the score is the Spearman correlation $\rho(\hat{\mathbf{y}}, \mathbf{y})$. On binary data, $\hat{y}_i$ is the distance-weighted fraction of neighbors in class 1, and the score is $2(\mathrm{AUROC} - 0.5)$, where AUROC is the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve. Both scores put chance at 0, which the budget split needs because it divides by the score. The score measures how far the similar property principle (Chapter~\ref{chap:background}) holds in the hybrid.

% [TRIM3 2026-09-30] simplified
% CODE INPUT
A fold with fewer than 10 held-out molecules, fewer than 2 scaffold groups, or constant labels or predictions is left out of the mean rather than scored 0. The similarity hybrid is not searched on three datasets. AUROC needs two classes, which excludes the PAM50 breast cancer subtypes (4 classes) and trastuzumab HER2 multiclass (3 classes). The folds of trastuzumab HER2 binary follow edit-distance shells around the wild-type CDR-H3, so a held-out block can hold a single shell, with no neighbor from another group. All other datasets are searched, including the antibody and other non-molecule datasets.


\paragraph{Feature importance weighting.}

Beyond simply using the feature importance scores for allocation, we investigate whether feature weighting by importance during distance computation improves noise detection. For a feature importance vector $w$, weighted distances are computed by scaling the standardized features: $x' = x \odot w$ under Manhattan distance ($x \odot \sqrt{w}$ under Euclidean), with negative importances set to 0 and $w$ rescaled to average 1. 


