% TODO: REVIEW MODIFICATION -- formula checked against the code on 2026-09-29: the experiments in this chapter compute recovery per downstream model, in the dataset's own metric, as a percentage, and leave it undefined when the clean-to-noisy gap is 2% of the clean score or less (tests/experiments/noise/n11_estimation_remediation_grid.py:300, 1457, 1511; same in n07, n32). The R^2 / random forest on ECFP4 version describes the exploratory study only (Table tab:code_input_neighbor_recovery). Your original paragraph is kept commented out below.
Each downstream model $m$ is trained on the clean, noisy and corrected training labels, giving held-out scores $s^{m}_{\text{clean}}$, $s^{m}_{\text{noisy}}$ and $s^{m}_{\text{denoised}}$ in the dataset's own metric, averaged over folds. The recovery rate is the share of the lost performance that correction recovers,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Recovery}_m = 100 \times \frac{s^{m}_{\text{denoised}} - s^{m}_{\text{noisy}}}{s^{m}_{\text{clean}} - s^{m}_{\text{noisy}}}\ \%,
\label{eq:recovery}
\end{equation}
which reads the same whether higher or lower values of the metric are better. When a model's gap between clean and noisy score is no more than $2\%$ of the clean score, the recovery rate is left undefined. A recovery rate of $100\%$ fully restores clean performance, and a negative value means the corrected labels scored worse than the noisy ones.
