The limits of the hybrid search itself are in the Limitations section of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}. This thesis set out to mitigate label noise, and we conclude that correcting it without ground truth is a hard problem with no clear general solution. There is no clean copy of the labels to check a correction against, and finding a noisy label is easier than correcting it (``Comparison across noise types'', Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}). Outliers and systematic errors of known structure, such as a shift shared by one source or clipping at a detection limit, can still be detected. In the assay experiments, ``clean'' means no noise was added, not that the labels are true; only QM9 is close to noise-free. Real data mix the injected noise conditions in unknown amounts, so a method that works under one condition may fail on real data. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: per noise condition, methods beating the uncorrected model; negative recovery.]}

