Noise mitigation writes an estimated correction into the labels before training, so a wrong estimate stays for every later model; it is cheaper, since it is done once and reused by any model. LNL methods that change training instead adapt throughout training and use all the data \citep{Song2022}. Correcting labels directly is justified when the noise has a known direction, as in censoring, or affects a clearly separate subset, such as gross outliers or an offset tied to one source. Otherwise, we recommend LNL methods that change training. Either approach can be paired with uncertainty estimation where it is reliable. Predicted uncertainty rises with noise (Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}), but per-molecule uncertainty does not identify noisy labels, so it should be used to judge how noisy a dataset is, not which labels are wrong.

